Beryllium: what a page reads back from WebGL carries the canvas noise

Purpose: Helium's canvas noise is called in four places: getImageData(),
  toDataURL(), toBlob() and OffscreenCanvas convertToBlob()
  (helium/core/noise/canvas.patch). WebGL's readPixels() is not one of
  them. A WebGL scene is the other picture fingerprinting scripts hash,
  and they read it with readPixels(); and a page can hand any 2D canvas to
  WebGL as a texture and read that. Measured with the battery on Beryllium
  0.20.3.17 on FreeBSD (freebsd/privacy/noise-hash/README.md, 11 November
  2026): a 2D canvas of known pixels copied into a texture came back from
  readPixels() with every pixel exact in 30 readbacks of 30, while
  getImageData() of the same canvas changed about 2,200 of its 2,304
  pixels. The same on Helium 0.21.2.1 on macOS and Fedora, 60 of 60.
  The change: WebGLRenderingContextBase::ReadPixelsHelper(), through which
  every readPixels() into an array goes in WebGL 1 and 2, hands what it
  read to a new CanvasNoisingHelper::MaybeNoiseWebGLReadback() when the
  read is RGBA and UNSIGNED_BYTE. That runs Helium's 8-bit noise over the
  pixels with the site's canvas token, anchored to the framebuffer
  coordinates of the read, so that two reads of one place agree and a
  read in pieces gets the noise of a read in one. It follows the pack
  parameters (alignment, and in WebGL 2 row length and skipped rows and
  pixels), so that none of them is a way round; a layout it cannot place
  in the destination is handed back as zeros.
  One thing it does not do as getImageData() does. Helium's noise moves
  about one pixel in four of a flat area by one level. WebGL pages read
  flat areas back for their exact values: the colour under the pointer
  names the object drawn there, and a page that gets a neighbouring value
  one time in four selects the wrong thing or nothing. (Tor Browser's
  readPixels() protection broke pages this way:
  gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/applications/tor-browser/-/issues/42082.) So
  a pixel whose whole 3 by 3 neighbourhood in the read is one exact colour
  is handed back as read. A flat area tells a page nothing one pixel of
  it would not, and Helium's sparse noise did not hide that pixel either:
  three in four of the area's pixels were exact already.
  What is still open, on purpose or for later (feature matrix P-FPR-07):
  - a read of one pixel at a time: each is a neighbourhood of itself, so
    each is exact. That is what keeps one-pixel picking working, and it
    costs a page one call a pixel;
  - a read in another format: RGBA with FLOAT or HALF_FLOAT, integer
    formats, or whatever second format the driver offers. Float reads are
    how pages take numbers out of the GPU, and the 8-bit noise clamps
    floats to colours;
  - WebGL 2 readPixels() into a buffer object, read with
    getBufferSubData();
  - a canvas copied into a video frame, captureStream, createImageBitmap.
  Costs: a read of w by h pixels is copied twice more and hashed, as
  getImageData() is. A page that packs numbers into bytes and reads them
  with RGBA and UNSIGNED_BYTE gets them noised unless its results are
  flat; the per-site exception to the noise is the way out. A multi-pixel
  pick that straddles the edge of an object gets noised edge pixels. And
  if the GL read itself fails, the bytes noised are the page's own.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on helium/core/noise/canvas.patch,
  which creates the helper, and on
  privacy/beryllium-noise-snapshot-channel-order.patch, which changes the
  same file before it.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not
  compiled in the tree. The new function, with the 8-bit path of the
  helper it calls, and the layout arithmetic added to ReadPixelsHelper()
  were compiled verbatim outside the tree against Chromium's own
  base/containers/span.h, base/numerics/checked_math.h and
  byte_conversions.h (clang 18, libc++, C++23, with -Wall -Wextra
  -Wshorten-64-to-32 -Wsign-compare -Wimplicit-int-conversion: no warning
  in the new code) and run on 240 cases of images, pack parameters,
  offsets and buffer sizes: the output is the Python model's
  (freebsd/privacy/noise-hash/model.py, webgl_readback) in every case;
  39 flat fills of 39 and 16 one-pixel reads of 16 came back exact; 9
  buffers too small for their layout came back as zeros. What that did
  not compile: the three lines that call the helper from the WebGL
  context (GetPackPixelStoreParams(), Host()->GetTopExecutionContext(),
  ByteSpanMaybeShared().subspan()), each used elsewhere in that file in
  the same form. First build: 0.20.3.18.
Test: freebsd/privacy/noise-hash/battery. On 0.20.3.17 "A canvas copied
  into a WebGL texture reads back exact, without noise" reads 30 of 30 and
  must read 0 of 30, with "reads back with the noise of getImageData" at
  30 of 30; "WebGL: single pixels and 3 by 3 blocks of a flat colour read
  back exact" must stay 30 of 30; "WebGL: read one pixel at a time, a
  canvas comes back exact" stays 30 of 30 and is the open route named
  above. The battery's self-test runs these rows against stand-in noise
  that behaves as this patch intends. Feature matrix row P-FPR-07.
security-review: not required for the sandbox (renderer-only, no new
  input from outside the page, no new privilege). It changes what a web
  API returns: a compatibility review is the first build's battery run and
  the owner's use of WebGL pages on it.

